<page type="text">
  <layout>One printed page, single column of running text; faint bleed-through from the facing page in the background (not transcribed). Running head "DE." at top centre; page number 196 at top left. No marginal notes, no catchword visible beyond the last line's final word "Descri-" which is a hyphenated line end.</layout>
  <page_number>196</page_number>
  <running_head>DE.</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
    <catchword>Descri-</catchword>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <p cont="true">didicit. Depudere didicit, <i>Sen.</i> Omnem partem pudoris contrivit, <i>Petr. Vict.</i> Præmortui pudoris homo, <i>Liv.</i> Veteri perdidit, <i>Plaut.</i></p>
    <p><i>Depulsor</i] Propulsator, <i>Val.M.</i> Prohibitor avium &amp; furum, <i>Macrob.</i></p>
    <p><i>Deradere aliquid</i>] Circumradere. Desquamare corpus arboris. Circumscarificare. Circumscalpere aliquid, <i>Plin.</i> Abradere dolabrâ quæ arida &amp; vetera sunt. Abradere vitem, arborem. Stringere arborem, <i>Col.</i> Descobinare, <i>Varr.</i> Radere tigna, <i>Lucr.</i> Circumfricare dolii labia. Scarificare radicem, <i>Cat.</i> Circumdolare, <i>Plin.</i> Muscus ferro distringendus, <i>Col.</i></p>
    <p><i>Derelinquere</i>, vid. <i>Deserere.</i></p>
    <p><i>Derepente</i>, vid. <i>Subitò.</i></p>
    <p><i>Deridere aliquem</i>] Ridere aliquem. Irridere. Ridiculum in aliquem jacere. Per ridiculum de absentibus dicere, <i>Cic.</i> Jactitare ridicula, <i>Liv.</i> Insultare aliquem, <i>Salust.</i> Vid. <i>Ludificare aliquem.</i></p>
    <p><i>Derisor</i>] Irrisor. Catagelasinus. Nolo è gelasino mihi te Catagelasinum, <i>Plaut.</i> Vid. <i>Cavillator.</i></p>
    <p><i>Derivare aquam</i>] Ducere, deducere aquam, <i>Cic.</i> Corrivare aquam, <i>Plin.</i> Ducere fossam, <i>Id.</i> Delibrare aquam, <i>Fest.</i></p>
    <p><i>Derogare</i> alicujus famæ. Vid. <i>Famæ derogare.</i></p>
    <p><i>Derogator</i>] Detractor laudum suarum, <i>Liv.</i></p>
    <p><i>Descendere</i>] Descensionem facere. Degredi ad pedes, <i>Liv.</i> Demeare suis pedibus ad tartarum, <i>Apul.</i></p>
    <p><i>Descendere in se</i>] Remetior dicta &amp; facta mea, <i>Sen.</i> Perspicere se penitus, totumque tentare. Inquirere in se, &amp; se observare. Introspicito in mentem tuam ipse, eamque omni cogitatione pertractans, percontare ipse te, <i>Cic.</i> Considerare secum in animo vitam suam, <i>Ter.</i> Recognosco vitam meam. Quotidie cum vita paria faciamus. Respicere vitam suam. Excutere se. Inquirere in se. Se colit animus. Ad computationem ætatem tuam revoca, <i>Sen.</i></p>
    <p><i>Desciscere</i>] Deficere ab aliquo ad aliquem. Deficere aliquem, <i>Cic.</i> Defectionem inire. Rebellare alicui, <i>Liv.</i> Abjicere &amp; relinquere obedientiam, <i>Cic.</i> Deserere virtutis viam, <i>Hor.</i></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Bleed-through from the facing page is visible in places and is not transcribed. The final line's "Hor." is a catchword-style reference at the foot left; the last word "Descri-" is the catchword at the foot right. Some words in the Greek-free Latin are uncertain due to fading, notably "Remetior" (possibly "Remetior") and "Demeare" in the middle of the page.</notes>
</page>